::GEO_U01_L05_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>What does a segment bisector do? {
=A. Divides a segment into two equal parts#Correct. A segment bisector creates two congruent segment parts.
~B. Copies a segment to a new ray#That describes copying a segment, not bisecting a segment.
~C. Measures an angle in degrees#A segment bisector works with segment length, not angle measure.
~D. Names the endpoints of a segment#Naming endpoints does not divide the segment into equal parts.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>If point M is the midpoint of segment AB, which statement must be true? {
~A. AM > MB#A midpoint creates equal parts, not one longer part.
=B. AM = MB#Correct. A midpoint divides a segment into two equal lengths.
~C. AB = MB#The whole segment is not equal to one of its parts.
~D. A = B#Points are locations, not segment lengths.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>What must be true about a perpendicular bisector of segment AB? {
~A. It only touches endpoint A#A perpendicular bisector crosses the segment at its midpoint.
~B. It copies AB onto another ray#Copying a segment is a different construction.
=C. It crosses AB at its midpoint and forms right angles#Correct. A perpendicular bisector both bisects the segment and is perpendicular to it.
~D. It makes AB longer#A bisector does not change the length of the original segment.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>If ray BD bisects &angle;ABC, which statement must be true? {
~A. m&angle;ABD > m&angle;DBC#An angle bisector creates equal angle measures.
=B. m&angle;ABD = m&angle;DBC#Correct. An angle bisector divides an angle into two congruent angles.
~C. AB = BC#Angle bisection does not require equal side lengths.
~D. BD = AB#Angle bisection does not require the ray length to equal a side.
}

::GEO_U01_L05_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L05_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Why should matching compass openings be used when constructing a bisector? {
~A. They make the diagram darker#The purpose is accuracy, not appearance.
~B. They replace the need for a straightedge#A straightedge is still needed to draw the final line or ray.
=C. They provide evidence that distances being transferred are equal#Correct. Equal compass openings support equal distances in the construction.
~D. They measure every angle in degrees#A compass transfers distance; it does not measure degrees.
}
